Experiment: Discover Live Hosts Using Nmap Scans on TryHackMe

Aim
To discover live hosts on a network using Nmap by performing ARP, ICMP, TCP, and UDP host discovery scans on an authorized TryHackMe lab network.

Requirements
- TryHackMe account
- Nmap tool
- Linux terminal or TryHackMe AttackBox
- Authorized target IP address or network range

Algorithm
1. Start the TryHackMe lab and launch the AttackBox or connect to the lab VPN.
2. Identify the target IP address or authorized network range.
3. Open the terminal and verify the Nmap installation using nmap --version.
4. Perform ARP-based host discovery on the local Ethernet network.
5. Perform ICMP-based discovery to identify hosts that respond to ping requests.
6. Perform TCP-based discovery using selected TCP ports.
7. Perform UDP-based discovery using selected UDP ports.
8. Observe the scan results and identify live hosts.
9. Compare the results of the different discovery techniques.
10. Stop the lab after completing the experiment.

Commands and Sample Output
1. Check Nmap Installation
nmap --version

Sample output:
Nmap version 7.x
Platform: x86_64-pc-linux-gnu

Observation Table
Scan type	Nmap option	Purpose
ARP	-PR	Discovers hosts on the local Ethernet network
ICMP	-PE	Discovers hosts using ICMP Echo Requests
TCP	-PS	Discovers hosts using TCP SYN probes
UDP	-PU	Discovers hosts using UDP probes


Result
Nmap host discovery techniques using ARP, ICMP, TCP, and UDP were studied. The scans demonstrate how different probe types can identify live hosts on an authorized network, including cases where some discovery methods are blocked.
